Alright, I'm still focused on improving the HUD rendering logic for the NeonStrike client. The recent series of edits to `hud.js` have already cleaned up imports, fixed the crosshair update flow, and made the minimap scaling more robust by pulling the world dimensions from `g.map` instead of the static constants. The next logical enhancement is to make the minimap more informative for multiplayer sessions.

Looking at the current minimap drawing code (the block I just edited for scaling), I see that after drawing the walls footprint, the script iterates over `g.bots` to plot their positions on the map:

```js
// bots
for (const b of g.bots) {
  if (!b.alive) continue;
  const [mx, my] = toMap(b.pos[0], b.pos[2]);
  ctx.fillStyle = '#ff4040';
  ctx.beginPath();
  ctx.arc(mx, my, 3, 0, Math.PI * 2);
  ctx.fill();
}
```

This works fine for local bots, but in a networked deathmatch the client also tracks remote players via `g.remoteTargets` (I recall seeing that in the networking code). Those remote entities have `x` and `z` coordinates (world positions) but aren't currently visualized on the minimap, which is a noticeable omission for the user. Adding a simple loop to draw those remote targets will give players a quick visual cue of where teammates or opponents are, matching the visual style already used for bots.

I need to be careful not to disturb any other parts of the file. The edit should be minimal: replace the exact block that draws bots with a slightly expanded version that also draws remote players. I’ll adjust the comment to reflect both categories, keep the existing bot rendering unchanged, and then append a new loop for `g.remoteTargets`. For the remote markers, I'll choose a distinct color (`#ffb040`) to differentiate them from bots (`#ff4040`). The geometry conversion stays the same: `toMap(r.x, r.z)`.

Since this is a straightforward textual substitution, I’ll use the `Edit` tool with `replace_all: false` targeting the exact old string block. This ensures I only modify the intended section. The new string will contain the original bot loop plus the added remote player loop, preserving the existing logic and adding the new feature.

Proceed with the edit.

